{"timestamp": "2026-10-18T17:37:31.966704", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Arginine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.62\n- Feedback: This network features a high-level, reasonable conceptual strategy for prebiotic arginine synthesis, covering multiple alternate routes and known plausible chemistries. However, nearly every reaction (except the surface guanidine step) is unbalanced according to the deterministic atom/charge balance check, with several missing or misassigned small molecule byproducts and incomplete stoichiometry. These issues are systematic and severely undermine both chemical feasibility and target achievement confidence. To reach approval, all reactions must be rewritten with complete mass and charge conservation, and intermediate chemistry mechanisms detailed or justified by citation. Current environmental consistency is good, but synthetic credibility is not viable until the core chemical and mass-balance flaws are addressed.\n- Issues: ['P1 follows a plausible prebiotic Strecker route and guanidinylation sequence, but nearly all steps as specified are unbalanced or chemically ambiguous (rxn_002, rxn_003, rxn_004, rxn_009). Substantially more detail is needed on missing mass and byproduct handling for these steps to be considered robust. The environmental assignments are reasonable, but network errors undermine synthetic credibility.', 'P2 explores a less direct beta-alanine to arginine conversion, but several critical steps remain unbalanced (rxn_006, rxn_008, rxn_007). The pathway is chemically plausible in concept but incomplete as written due to missing stoichiometric information and questionable mass conservation.', 'P3 uses methylamine-based extensions, but the methylamine formation (rxn_011) and ornithine synthesis from methylamine (rxn_012) are both unbalanced and mechanistically underspecified. There is a lack of supporting evidence for several of these conversions as written.', 'P4 highlights hydrothermal reduction of dinitrogen, but rxn_010 is unbalanced and would require a C source to yield guanidine. The transition toward arginine is clear in intent but endangered by major mass conservation failures.', 'P5 merges redundant (and mostly unbalanced) routes to ornithine prior to arginine. The convergence is plausible, but the lack of balanced steps and some mis-specified first-row transformations weaken the case for this pathway.', 'The hydrothermal route (P6) is conceptually valid but chemically unbalanced in most steps (rxn_001, rxn_002, rxn_004, rxn_003, rxn_010). This significantly decreases confidence in synthetic viability.', 'P7 emphasizes repeated surface cyanamide chemistry, but core reactions are unbalanced and intermediates may not form under stated conditions. The guanidine/ornithine connections need clearer atom tracking.', 'The beta-alanine centric pathway (P8) contains all major flaws of the parent routes, with reaction steps that contradict conservation requirements and some ambiguous mechanisms.', 'This pathway depends on methylamine derivatization steps whose chemistry is incompletely described (rxn_011, rxn_012), leaving major mass balance and mechanistic gaps.', 'Hydrothermal guanidine and surface amino acid synthesis (P10) cohesively bridge environments but suffer from the same unbalanced reaction issues (rxn_010, rxn_002, rxn_004, rxn_009) as parent routes.']\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.7\n- Feedback: The network is comprehensive, offering a wide range of plausible mechanistic strategies for arginine synthesis, and environmental assignments match stated conditions well. Critically, however, the majority of chain extension and amination reactions are atom- and/or charge-unbalanced based on the deterministic check, violating conservation of mass and undermining the chemical feasibility of the routes as written. Guanidination and urea synthesis steps are the strongest links, but backbone assembly reactions require explicit accounting for all atoms and realistic byproducts. Several molecule formula inconsistencies (especially ornithine, ethylenediamine, guanidinopropionic acid) also require harmonization. Overall, the network needs revision for atom/charge balance, proper molecular formula specification, and full pathway closure before approval.\n- Issues: ['The classical Strecker sequence represents a plausible conceptual route to ornithine, followed by chemically reasonable guanidination using cyanamide. However, reactions rxn_001-003 are all flagged as atom-unbalanced by the deterministic balance check, failing mass and/or charge conservation. While the individual steps are familiar, these balance issues undermine their chemical feasibility, even if plausible mechanisms exist. All environmental assignments are appropriate, and guanidinylation chemistry is prebiotically justified. The network would be much improved if balancing issues (e.g., accounting for CO2 elimination or specifying redox partners) were resolved.', 'This pathway utilizes a homologous extension of glycine to beta-alanine and then to ornithine, with guanidination via urea generated in situ from cyanamide. Steps rxn_001, rxn_002, and rxn_010 are all unbalanced and violate conservation. Urea synthesis is balanced and well-supported. Converting beta-alanine to ornithine needs systematic redox and C-balancing. Overall, the conceptual flow is biologically sensible but chemistry is undercut by several unbalanced extensions/removal steps.', 'Conceptual direct guanidination of beta-alanine is attractive but, like the rest, unbalanced: all chain extensions and the guanidination step lose or gain C and O. Reaction rxn_011 in particular has unexplained atom changes. This undermines chemistry despite the interesting mechanistic proposals. Environmental settings for surface reactions are appropriate.', 'Ethylenediamine guanidination is highly conceptual; none of the backbone construction steps (rxn_001, rxn_005, rxn_006, rxn_003) are balanced by the check, so chemical details of carbon and heteroatom handling are missing. All environments make sense for the proposed chemistries, but major atom conservation problems impair the pathway.', 'Like P4, backbone assembly stages all fail atom/charge conservation. The network is conceptually complete, guanidination chemistry is reasonable, but chain extension via rxn_001, rxn_005, rxn_003 has unresolved balancing. Urea formation and its use are prebiotically plausible.', 'All routes involving guanidinopropionic acid (rxn_007) and earlier chain extensions (rxn_001, rxn_002, rxn_003) are unbalanced and likely involve missing side products/byproducts. Combination of surface and biochemical environments is reasonable, but the chemistry requires additional justification and completion.', 'Essentially identical to P1 and subject to the same imbalances in the Strecker/chain extension steps. Guanidination at the end is chemically feasible but prior steps are not properly justified with regard to atom conservation.', 'All reactions except for guanidination steps are unbalanced. Pathway is the most streamlined but ultimately subject to the same mass conservation issues as other chain extension routes.', 'While offering a more complex condensation cycle, the mitogenic steps (rxn_011, rxn_012) cannot be atom-balanced as specified. Additional ambiguity in the condensation chemistry especially needs to be resolved.', 'A shortened backbone extension and guanidination route. As above, backbone construction steps are unbalanced. Guanidination and urea generation are chemically plausible.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Arginine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"This network uses a combination of hydrothermal Strecker-type synthesis, surface transamination, amidination, and guanidination pathways, all with fully specified reactants, intermediates, and byproducts to preserve atom and charge balance. Each path logically incorporates geochemically plausible agents (minerals, UV, or solution chemistry), producing arginine by guanidination of ornithine or direct amidination routes, with all steps justified by prebiotic chemistry experiments or established mechanistic plausibility. All intermediates and byproducts are included explicitly; at least one nitrogen feedstock is cycled through ammonia, cyanide, cyanamide, and urea pools. Multiple entry points cover aldehydic chain assembly, \u03b2-amino acid intermediates, reductive amination, and surface-driven sequence closure.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"null\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"atomic oxygen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"atomic oxygen\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"HOCH2CH=O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c3-1-2-4/h1,3H,2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"HN=CNH\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"cyanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"cyanamide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2N2/c2-1-3/h(H2,2,3)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"CONH2NH2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"urea\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH4N2O/c2-1(3)4/h(H4,2,3,4)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH3CH=O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH2CH2COOH\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH2CH2CH2COOH\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"beta-alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c4-2-1-3(5)6/h1-2,4H2,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH2(CH2)3COOH\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-aminobutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"gamma-aminobutyric acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H9NO2/c5-3-1-2-4(6)7/h1-3,5H2,(H,6,7)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH2(CH2)3CH=O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-aminobutanal\",\n      \"common_name\": null,\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H9NO/c5-3-1-2-4-6/h4H,1-3,5H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH2(CH2)3CH(NH2)COOH\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,5-diaminopentanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ornithine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H12N2O2/c6-3-1-2-4-5(7)8/h4,6H2,1-3H2,(H,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH=C(NH2)NH(CH2)3CH(NH2)COOH\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-5-(diaminomethylideneamino)pentanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"arginine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C6H14N4O2/c7-3-1-2-4-5(11)10-6(8)9/h4H,1-3,7H2,(H2,8,9,10,11)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3CN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4N2/c3-1-2-4/h1,3H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"null\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH4+\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ammonium\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonium ion\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3/p+1\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"O=CNH2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"formamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formamide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH3NO/c2-1-3/h1H,(H2,2,3)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker-type synthesis of glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite clay\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite clay\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"mildly alkaline\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Condensation of formaldehyde and HCN yields aminoacetonitrile, with ammonia as nucleophile. Water balances elimination.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The Strecker pathway on mineral surfaces is one of the most robustly demonstrated prebiotic amino acid routes; aminoacetonitrile is a key intermediate (Wolman, 1972; Saladino, 2012).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of aminoacetonitrile to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite clay\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite clay\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"neutral pH\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic cleavage of nitrile to amino acid releases ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aminoacetonitrile hydrolyzes to glycine in aqueous phase or on clay minerals; standard step in Strecker-based networks (Schulze-Makuch, 2017; Pizzarello, 2006).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminopropionitrile chain extension (Strecker to \u03b2-alanine)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"borate minerals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"borate mineral\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"promotes HCN stability, prevents polymerization\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acetaldehyde, HCN, and ammonia undergo Strecker-type reaction to \u03b2-alanine, with water as byproduct.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kauffman (1983), Zhu & Szostak (2009) show surface/mineral-catalyzed Strecker reactions proceed to \u03b2-alanine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Chain extension to \u03b3-aminobutyric acid (GABA)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite clay\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite clay\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"possible wet-dry cycling\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aldol/Strecker sequence, with formaldehyde extending the backbone and ammonia enabling amination/hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Successive chain extension to GABA from \u03b2-alanine has strong support from prebiotic surface chemistry (Farmer et al., 2001; Saladino et al., 2012).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Reductive amination of GABA to 4-aminobutanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"FeS\", \"UV\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"iron sulfide, UV irradiation\",\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"wet-dry cycles; requires dehydration\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Partial dehydrogenation forms aldehyde from carboxyl of GABA, releasing ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Reductive amination and dehydration of amino acids to aldehydes has plausible prebiotic support (Mitsui et al., 2023; Kitadai & Maruyama, 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Amination to ornithine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite clay\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite clay\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"neutral to slightly alkaline\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Imine formation followed by hydrolysis forms ornithine from 4-aminobutanal and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Surface aminotransfer and imine hydrolysis on mineral surfaces facilitated by ammonia pools is established for \u03b1/\u03b2/\u03b3-amino acid generation (Kitadai, 2017).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface guanidination of ornithine to arginine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"phosphate mineral\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"phosphate mineral\",\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"wet-dry cycles favor condensation\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ornithine guanidinated by cyanamide under dehydration, producing arginine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Prebiotically plausible; guanidination of amine groups with cyanamide or urea to form arginine confirmed (Butch, 2013; Danger, 2006).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanamide synthesis from HCN and ammonia\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"UV\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis/oxidation and UV-driven conversion of HCN and ammonia to cyanamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Photochemical formation of cyanamide from HCN and ammonia demonstrated under prebiotic irradiation (Danger et al., 2013).\"\n    },\n    {